  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a two-part physical picture of how a single object or state can move between different energy landscapes and interact with a nearby platform. On the left, potential energy is plotted against position, with two distinct red potential surfaces: an upper, funnel-like landscape and a lower, bowl-shaped one. Blue irregular shapes appear to represent different configurations or localized states within these potentials. Curved arrows indicate motion or transitions within each landscape and also a transfer from the upper surface into the lower one, suggesting relaxation between states or trapping in lower-energy minima.\n\nOn the right, the schematic is simplified into a model with two quantized wells connected through a central element labeled S, positioned above a larger red body labeled T. The two wells have visible energy levels, and the labels “energy difference” and “coupling strength” indicate the main tunable quantities controlling the interaction. Wavy lines connect the wells and the central object to the red substrate-like element, implying coupling to an external environment or support. Overall, the figure conveys a transition from a complex spatial energy landscape to a reduced model emphasizing state energies and coupling to a target or substrate.",
